var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"guide/constrained/#Constrained-optimization-and-AD","page":"Constrained optimization and AD","text":"(Image: Initial design)\n\nAdd member areas as variables, and minimize material volume subject to displacement and stress limits. Without the constraints the trivial optimum is a flat truss of zero area; with them, depth trades against member force. This continues the Quick start model:\n\nvars = AbstractVariable[\n    [SpatialVariable(node, 0.0, xmin, xmax, :X) for node in model.nodes[:top]];\n    [SpatialVariable(node, 0.0, ymin, ymax, :Y) for node in model.nodes[:top]];\n    [AreaVariable(element, element.section.A, 0.01 * element.section.A, 5 * element.section.A)\n     for element in model.elements]\n]\n\nparams = OptParams(model, vars)\nx0 = copy(params.values)\n\n# objective: material volume (geometry only, no solve needed)\nfunction obj(x, p)\n    geo = GeometricProperties(x, p)\n    dot(geo.L, geo.A)\nend\nOBJ = x -> obj(x, params)\n\n# constraints: g(x) .<= 0 is feasible (Nonconvex.jl convention), each row\n# normalized by its limit, because mixed-scale constraint rows stall MMA.\n# DOF layout: 6 DOFs per node, so vertical displacements are U[2:6:end]\nfunction cstr(x, p, dmax, smax)\n    res = solve_structure(x, p)\n    return [\n        abs.(res.U[2:6:end]) ./ dmax .- 1.0;\n        abs.(axial_stress(res, p)) ./ smax .- 1.0\n    ]\nend\nCSTR = x -> cstr(x, params, dmax, fy)\n\noptmodel = Nonconvex.Model(OBJ)\naddvar!(optmodel, params.lb, params.ub)\nadd_ineq_constraint!(optmodel, CSTR)\n\nres = optimize(optmodel, NLoptAlg(:LD_MMA), x0,\n    options = NLoptOptions(maxeval = 500, maxtime = 60))\n\nmodel2 = updatemodel(params, res.minimizer)\n\n(Image: Optimized design)","title":"Constrained optimization and AD"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/constrained/#AD-backends:-reverse-for-objectives,-forward-for-constraints","page":"Constrained optimization and AD","text":"Loading Zygote (or anything ChainRulesCore-aware) is all the setup reverse-mode AD needs: Asap's rule extension activates automatically, and this package has no custom rules. ForwardDiff, Mooncake and Enzyme (both modes) also work.\n\nThe rule of thumb, from the benchmarks in docs/AD_BACKENDS.md:\n\nScalar objectives (compliance, volume): reverse mode, with Zygote or Enzyme. The cost is about one extra adjoint solve, independent of the number of design variables.\nConstraint Jacobians (per-element stresses, nodal displacements): forward mode, with ForwardDiff. A forward Jacobian costs the same as a forward gradient, because extra outputs are free. On constraint blocks it beats reverse mode by two orders of magnitude: the 512×512 stress Jacobian of the benchmark spaceframe takes 43 ms with ForwardDiff and 7.6 s with Zygote.\n\nThis holds whenever there are at least as many constraint rows as design variables, which describes most sized or shaped structural problems, since stresses are checked in every element.","title":"AD backends: reverse for objectives, forward for constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/constrained/#Choosing-the-linear-solver","page":"Constrained optimization and AD","text":"OptParams takes a solver keyword that applies to every solve through the params: values, reverse-mode adjoints and forward-mode tangents alike.\n\nparams = OptParams(model, vars)                               # built-in CHOLMOD\nparams = OptParams(model, vars; solver = KLUFactorization())  # any LinearSolve.jl algorithm\nparams = OptParams(model, vars; solver = Asap.CachedSolver()) # factorization sharing (below)","title":"Choosing the linear solver"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/constrained/#Sharing-work-between-the-objective-and-the-constraints","page":"Constrained optimization and AD","text":"At each optimizer iterate, the objective gradient, the constraint Jacobian and every ForwardDiff chunk assemble and factorize the same stiffness matrix. Asap.CachedSolver() makes them share one factorization. It keys on the stiffness values, reuses the factorization when they are unchanged, and refactorizes numerically (reusing the symbolic analysis) when they move. Your objective and constraint code does not change; it is purely a params setting. Use one CachedSolver per OptParams. Standard AD drivers and optimizer loops satisfy its documented atomicity contract automatically.\n\nThe complete pattern, with mixed AD, a shared factorization and NLopt driven directly, is examples/truss-optimization3-nlopt.jl. Distilled:\n\nusing NLopt, Zygote, ForwardDiff, DifferentiationInterface\n\nparams = OptParams(model, vars; solver = Asap.CachedSolver())\nx0 = copy(params.values)\n\nvolume(x) = begin geo = GeometricProperties(x, params); dot(geo.L, geo.A) end\nconstraints(x) = begin                      # g(x) .<= 0, rows normalized by limits\n    res = solve_structure(x, params)\n    [abs.(res.U[2:6:end]) ./ dmax .- 1.0; abs.(axial_stress(res, params)) ./ fy .- 1.0]\nend\n\n# reverse mode for the scalar objective\nfunction nlopt_objective(x, grad)\n    v, g = Zygote.withgradient(volume, x)\n    length(grad) > 0 && (grad .= g[1])\n    return v\nend\n\n# prepared FORWARD mode for the constraint Jacobian\nbackend = AutoForwardDiff()\nprep = prepare_jacobian(constraints, backend, x0)\nfunction nlopt_constraints!(result, x, grad)   # NLopt wants grad = J' (n × m)\n    v, J = value_and_jacobian(constraints, prep, backend, x)\n    result .= v\n    length(grad) > 0 && (grad .= J')\nend\n\nopt = Opt(:LD_MMA, length(x0))\nNLopt.lower_bounds!(opt, params.lb); NLopt.upper_bounds!(opt, params.ub)\nNLopt.min_objective!(opt, nlopt_objective)\nNLopt.inequality_constraint!(opt, nlopt_constraints!, fill(1e-8, length(constraints(x0))))\nminf, minx, ret = optimize(opt, x0)\n\nOn the example-2 problem this runs 500 MMA iterations in about 7 s, where the all-Zygote Nonconvex formulation needs about 60 s. To stay in Nonconvex.jl, wrap the same per-function derivatives with Nonconvex.CustomGradFunction(f, g) instead.","title":"Sharing work between the objective and the constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/constrained/#Even-faster-constraint-Jacobians:-implicit-differentiation","page":"Constrained optimization and AD","text":"For the common constraint quantities, solution_tangents(x, params) computes the exact solution tangents ∂U/∂x by the implicit-function theorem, with one factorization and one multi-RHS back-substitution. It is about 10× faster than the prepared ForwardDiff Jacobian (3.9 ms against 43 ms on a 512-element, 512-variable benchmark) with about 20× less allocation:\n\nt = solution_tangents(x, params)        # res + ∂U/∂x, exact\nJ = [\n    -t.dU[3:6:end, :];                  # vertical-displacement rows\n    axial_stress_jacobian(t, params)    # stress rows\n]\n\nIt works for any mix of spatial, area, section, joint variables and couplings, including members with element loads, whose fixed-end forces enter the tangents. It is verified to machine precision against ForwardDiff and Zygote, and it respects params.solver. It is a plain-Float64 evaluation path for optimizer callbacks, not itself differentiable.\n\nEnzyme notes: reverse mode works (examples/ad_backends/). Forward mode is verified correct on Julia 1.11 with the default solver and is a useful independent check, but ForwardDiff is faster for Jacobians. See docs/AD_BACKENDS.md for the current per-version status.","title":"Even faster constraint Jacobians: implicit differentiation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/variables/#Design-variables","page":"Design variables","text":"All variables share the pattern Variable(target, start_value, lower_bound, upper_bound, ...). They have one of two semantics:\n\nVariable Controls Semantics\nSpatialVariable(node, value, lb, ub, axis) node position along :X/:Y/:Z additive: the design entry is an offset from the modeled position (start at 0.0 to begin from the current geometry)\nSpatialVariable(node, vector, value, lb, ub) node position along an arbitrary directional \"rail\" additive: vector is normalized, so the design entry is arc length along the rail\nSectionVariable(element, value, lb, ub, prop) a section property: :A, :Ix, :Iy or :J (flexural and torsional properties require a FrameElement) absolute: the design entry replaces the property; the rest of the section is kept\nAreaVariable(element, value, lb, ub) cross-section area absolute: the design entry replaces the area; Ix, Iy, J and the material are kept\nJointVariable(element, position, value, lb, ub) rotational end-spring stiffness ky = kz at :start, :end or :both of a FrameElement absolute [force·length/rad]: semi-rigid connection design\nQVariable(element, value, lb, ub) force density of an FDMelement absolute: for force-density networks\n\nNotes:\n\nAreaVariable requires a geometric Section. A RigiditySection has no area to vary; parameterize its rigidities directly instead.\nMembers may carry element loads under any variable: the fixed-end forces follow the design (see Element loads).","title":"Design variables"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/variables/#Coupling-variables:-symmetry-and-grouping","page":"Design variables","text":"CoupledVariable(target, parent, factor = 1.0) makes target share parent's design-vector entry, scaled by factor. This expresses equality constraints for free: the design space shrinks instead of gaining constraint rows.\n\n# mirror-symmetric geometry: the right node's x-offset is the negative of the left's\nx_left = SpatialVariable(left_node, 0.0, -1.0, 1.0, :X)\nx_right = CoupledVariable(right_node, x_left, -1.0)\n\n# member groups: all web elements share one area\na_web = AreaVariable(web_elements[1], 0.01, 1e-4, 0.05)\ngroups = [CoupledVariable(el, a_web) for el in web_elements[2:end]]\n\n# joint pairs: couple another element's end to a JointVariable\n# (the target may be an element, or an (element, position) tuple)\nj = JointVariable(beam1, :start, 1e8, 1e5, 1e12)\nmirror = CoupledVariable((beam2, :end), j)\n\nAlways couple to the independent parent; chains of couplings are rejected.\n\nVariable types mix freely in one problem. Spatial, area, section-property and joint-stiffness variables, plus any couplings, can drive a single OptParams on models that mix frame and truss elements. Every combination is gradient-tested against finite differences in the test suite.","title":"Coupling variables: symmetry and grouping"},{"category":"section","location":"api/results/#Results-and-evaluation","page":"Results and evaluation","text":"Evaluating a design vector (solve_structure, GeometricProperties), the results it returns, the reductions over them (compliance, axial_force, axial_stress), and materializing a design back into a model (updatemodel).\n\nAsapOptim extends two of Asap's accessors on OptResults (the functions belong to Asap, so they are listed here by method):","title":"Results and evaluation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/results/#AsapOptim.GeometricProperties","page":"Results and evaluation","text":"GeometricProperties(x, p::OptParams)\n\nGeometry-only evaluation of a design — positions, lengths, areas — with NO structural solve. Use for objectives like material volume (dot(geo.A, geo.L)) where paying for a solve would be waste.\n\nFields\n\nX::Matrix, L::Vector, A::Vector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.GeometricProperties"},{"category":"type","location":"api/results/#AsapOptim.OptResults","page":"Results and evaluation","text":"OptResults\n\nOutputs of one design evaluation (solve_structure), carrying everything downstream objectives and constraints consume — all plain data, so gradients of any scalar of these fields flow back to the design vector.\n\nFields\n\nU::Vector: full-space displacements, 6 slots per node in the order ux, uy, uz, θx, θy, θz — node i's translations are U[6(i−1) .+ (1:3)] (e.g. all z-displacements are U[3:6:end])\nX::Matrix: evaluated node positions (3 × n_nodes)\nA::Vector: evaluated per-element areas\nL::Vector: evaluated element lengths\nsections::AbstractVector: the evaluated per-element sections (feed to further pure computations if needed)\nEA::AbstractVector: evaluated axial rigidities (plain vector — cheap under reverse AD)\nF::Vector: the full-space load vector the design was solved for — P − Pf(x) with design-dependent element loads (p.loads = :design), else the base-design p.F\nends: the evaluated per-element EndConditions (joint variables), or nothing when the problem has none\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.OptResults"},{"category":"method","location":"api/results/#AsapOptim.axial_stress-Tuple{OptResults, OptParams}","page":"Results and evaluation","text":"axial_stress(res::OptResults, p::OptParams) -> Vector\n\nPer-element axial stress [force/length²]: axial force over evaluated area.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.axial_stress"},{"category":"method","location":"api/results/#AsapOptim.solve_structure-Tuple{AbstractVector, OptParams}","page":"Results and evaluation","text":"solve_structure(x, p::OptParams) -> OptResults\n\nEvaluate a design vector: scatter positions and areas, build the load vector (element-load fixed-end forces re-evaluated at the design when p.loads = :design), run Asap's pure solve on the resulting ModelState, and package the results. Fully differentiable — Zygote.gradient(x -> f(solve_structure(x, p)), x0) needs nothing else, and the gradient includes the load path.\n\nsolve_truss and solve_frame are aliases (the unified core needs no distinction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.solve_structure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/results/#AsapOptim.updatemodel-Tuple{OptParams, AbstractVector}","page":"Results and evaluation","text":"updatemodel(p::OptParams, x) -> Model\n\nMaterialize a design vector back into the reference Asap.Model: write the evaluated node positions and element sections into the model, re-solve, and return it. The returned model is the ordinary mutable definition object — ready for post-processing, force recovery, visualization, or export.\n\n(The model's topology is untouched, so the frozen analysis cache is reused; this is a numeric re-assembly plus one factorization.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.updatemodel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/results/#Asap.compliance-Tuple{OptResults, OptParams}","page":"Results and evaluation","text":"compliance(res::OptResults, p::OptParams) -> Real\n\nExternal work Fᵀu of the evaluated design — the canonical smooth stiffness objective. F is the design's own load vector (res.F): with design-dependent element loads it varies with the design, and the gradient includes that term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Asap.compliance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/results/#Asap.axial_force-Tuple{OptResults, OptParams}","page":"Results and evaluation","text":"axial_force(res::OptResults, p::OptParams) -> Vector\n\nPer-element axial force [force], tension-positive, matching Asap's stateful axial_force(res, el) (slot 7 of the local end forces f = k·u_local + q̃: the axial action at the END node): EA/L times the axial elongation, plus the axial fixed-end force q̃₇ of any element loads on the member (an inclined loaded beam, self-weight on a column, …), evaluated at the design when p.loads = :design. Pure and differentiable; exact for truss elements and for frame elements with rigid axial connections.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Asap.axial_force"},{"category":"section","location":"api/variables/#Variables","page":"Variables","text":"Design variables, couplings, and the abstract variable type.","title":"Variables"},{"category":"type","location":"api/variables/#AsapOptim.AbstractVariable","page":"Variables","text":"AbstractVariable\n\nSupertype of design variables. Each independent variable owns one entry of the design vector x; a CoupledVariable shares its parent's entry (optionally scaled), which is how symmetry and other equality constraints are expressed without shrinking the design space by hand.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.AbstractVariable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/variables/#AsapOptim.AreaVariable","page":"Variables","text":"AreaVariable <: AbstractVariable\n\nA cross-section area variable: the design entry REPLACES the element section's area (absolute semantics — the starting value is the initial area). The section's other geometric properties (Ix, Iy, J) and material are kept; only elements with a geometric Section can carry an area variable (a RigiditySection has no area to vary — parameterize its rigidities directly instead).\n\nFields\n\nelement: the controlled element (FrameElement or TrussElement)\nvalue: starting area [length²]\nlb, ub: bounds [length²]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.AreaVariable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/variables/#AsapOptim.CoupledVariable","page":"Variables","text":"CoupledVariable <: AbstractVariable\n\nA variable that mirrors an independent parent variable's design entry, scaled by factor — e.g. symmetric node pairs (factor = −1 for mirrored x-coordinates), or member groups sharing one area.\n\nFields\n\ntarget: the coupled node or element\nparent::AbstractVariable: the independent variable whose entry is shared\nfactor::Float64: multiplier applied to the parent's entry\n\nExamples\n\njulia> parent = SpatialVariable(left_node, 0.0, -1.0, 1.0, :X)\n\njulia> CoupledVariable(right_node, parent, -1.0)   # mirror across the axis\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.CoupledVariable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/variables/#AsapOptim.JointVariable","page":"Variables","text":"JointVariable <: AbstractVariable\n\nA semi-rigid connection stiffness variable: the design entry REPLACES the rotational end-spring stiffness (ky = kz, both bending planes) of a frame element's connection at one or both ends [force·length/rad].\n\nStiffer joints cost more to fabricate (welding, bolts, embedments) — this variable is what lets an optimizer trade connection cost against structural performance. Axial and torsional connection stiffnesses keep the element's existing values.\n\nFields\n\nelement::FrameElement: the element whose connection is controlled\nposition::Symbol: :start, :end, or :both\nvalue, lb, ub: starting stiffness and bounds [force·length/rad]\n\nExample\n\njulia> JointVariable(beam, :both, 1e5, 1e3, 1e8)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.JointVariable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/variables/#AsapOptim.SectionVariable","page":"Variables","text":"SectionVariable <: AbstractVariable\n\nA geometric section-property variable: the design entry REPLACES one of the element section's properties (absolute semantics, like AreaVariable). property is :A, :Ix, :Iy, or :J — :A is exactly an AreaVariable; the flexural/torsional properties require a FrameElement (they do nothing on a truss). The section's other properties and material are kept.\n\nFields\n\nelement: the controlled element (FrameElement, or TrussElement for :A)\nvalue, lb, ub: starting value and bounds [length⁴, or length² for :A]\nproperty::Symbol: :A, :Ix, :Iy, or :J\n\nConstructors\n\njulia> SectionVariable(beam, 8e7, 1e6, 5e8, :Ix)\n\njulia> SectionVariable(beam, 1e6, 5e8, :Ix)   # starting value = current section value\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.SectionVariable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/variables/#AsapOptim.SpatialVariable","page":"Variables","text":"SpatialVariable <: AbstractVariable\n\nA nodal position variable: the design entry ADDS to the node's base position along a direction — a global axis, or an arbitrary directional \"rail\" (legacy semantics preserved — a value of 0 leaves the node at its modeled position):\n\nx′ = x₀ + value · direction\n\nThe rail direction is NORMALIZED at construction, so value/lb/ub are arc-length along the rail [length] regardless of the vector you pass.\n\nFields\n\nnode::Node: the controlled node\nvalue: starting perturbation along the direction [length]\nlb, ub: bounds on the perturbation [length]\ndirection::SVector{3,Float64}: unit direction of travel\n\nConstructors\n\njulia> SpatialVariable(node, 0.0, -1.0, 1.0, :Z)          # global axis (:X/:Y/:Z)\n\njulia> SpatialVariable(node, [1.0, 1.0, 0.0], 0.0, -1.0, 1.0)  # rail: ±1 along the diagonal\n\njulia> SpatialVariable(node, [1.0, 1.0, 0.0], -1.0, 1.0)       # rail, starting value 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.SpatialVariable"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/examples/#Examples","page":"Examples","text":"Runnable scripts in the repository's examples/ folder:\n\nFile What it shows\nexamples/truss-optimization1.jl geometry optimization with a compliance objective (the Quick start)\nexamples/truss-optimization2.jl constrained minimum volume: geometry and sizing, with MMA\nexamples/truss-optimization3-nlopt.jl the recommended pattern: NLopt driven directly, a reverse-mode objective, a forward-mode constraint Jacobian and CachedSolver (about 9× faster per run than the all-Zygote formulation)\nexamples/vierendeel-geometry.jl frame optimization: a Vierendeel truss profile, volume-scaled compliance, hand-rolled projected gradient descent\nexamples/joint-stiffness.jl JointVariable: the cheapest distribution of semi-rigid connection stiffness that meets a drift limit\nexamples/ad_backends/ the same problem differentiated with Zygote, Mooncake and Enzyme\n\nEngineering reports with full benchmark tables are in the repository's docs/ folder: AD backend comparisons, AD verification against the published implementation, and the publication gb_mma benchmark.","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/element-loads/#Element-loads","page":"Element loads","text":"Members may carry element loads: LineLoad/DistributedLoad, PointLoad, PointMoment and SelfWeight. Asap lowers them to fixed-end forces, and those forces depend on the design:\n\nFixed-end forces depend on Through Variables that reach them\nmember length and orientation integration along the member; local–global rotation SpatialVariable on the member's nodes\nρA SelfWeight AreaVariable, SectionVariable(:A)\nend springs and EI condensation of released or semi-rigid ends JointVariable, SectionVariable(:Ix/:Iy)\n\nSince AsapOptim 1.2 (with Asap 1.2), the load vector is re-evaluated at every design, F(x) = P − Pf(x). Gradients therefore include the load path, and they match finite differences of rebuilt, re-solved models. Earlier versions froze the load vector at the base design, which gave silently wrong gradients for all three rows above.","title":"Element loads"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/element-loads/#Semantics-under-design-change","page":"Element loads","text":"LineLoad / DistributedLoad are per-unit-length intensities over fractions of the member, so the total load follows the member's current length. This suits self-weight-like and tributary loads.\nPointLoad / PointMoment keep their fractional position, so they ride with the member.\nSelfWeight follows the current ρA and length.\n:local load directions rotate with the member; :global ones do not.\nNodal loads (NodeForce, NodeMoment) are constant.\n\nThe condensed end moments of a uniformly loaded beam with symmetric end springs κ are (wL²/12) · κL / (κL + 2EI). They run from 0 (hinged) to wL²/12 (clamped) across the range a joint optimizer explores, so joint-stiffness design on gravity-loaded frames needs this load path.","title":"Semantics under design change"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/element-loads/#Member-forces","page":"Element loads","text":"axial_force(res, params) adds the element loads' axial fixed-end force to the elastic term. This matches Asap's stateful axial_force(res, el): the axial action at the end node, tension-positive. It matters for inclined loaded members and for columns under self-weight. axial_stress and the implicit Jacobians (axial_force_jacobian, axial_stress_jacobian) include the same term.","title":"Member forces"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/element-loads/#Keeping-the-loads-fixed","page":"Element loads","text":"For studies where the load is fixed in space, compile the parameters with loads = :frozen:\n\nparams = OptParams(model, vars; loads = :frozen)\n\nThe base-design load vector params.F is then used for every design. Problems without element loads behave identically either way and pay nothing extra.","title":"Keeping the loads fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/evaluating/#Evaluating-designs","page":"Evaluating designs","text":"","title":"Evaluating designs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/evaluating/#solve_structure(x,-params)-OptResults","page":"Evaluating designs","text":"solve_structure is the full differentiable analysis. It scatters the design vector into positions, sections and joint stiffnesses, builds the load vector, runs Asap's pure solve, and packages the results. solve_truss and solve_frame are aliases: the core is unified, so one function serves trusses, frames and mixed models.\n\nOptResults fields are all plain data, so any scalar function of them is differentiable back to x:\n\nU: the global displacement vector, 6 DOFs per node (ux, uy, uz, θx, θy, θz). Node i's translations are U[6(i-1) .+ (1:3)], all y-displacements are U[2:6:end], and all z-displacements are U[3:6:end].\nX: evaluated node positions (3 × n_nodes).\nA, L: per-element areas and lengths.\nsections, EA: evaluated sections and axial rigidities.\nF: the load vector the design was solved for. It varies with the design when members carry element loads.\nends: evaluated end conditions (joint variables), or nothing.\n\nBuilt-in reductions of an OptResults:\n\ncompliance(res, params): external work Fᵀu, the canonical smooth stiffness objective.\naxial_force(res, params): per-element axial force, tension-positive, including the axial component of element loads.\naxial_stress(res, params): axial force over the evaluated area.","title":"solve_structure(x, params) -> OptResults"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/evaluating/#GeometricProperties(x,-params)","page":"Evaluating designs","text":"GeometricProperties is a geometry-only evaluation, returning positions X, lengths L and areas A with no structural solve. Use it for objectives like material volume (dot(geo.A, geo.L)), where paying for a solve and its gradient would be waste.","title":"GeometricProperties(x, params)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/evaluating/#updatemodel(params,-x)-Model","page":"Evaluating designs","text":"updatemodel materializes a design back into the reference Asap.Model. It writes positions, sections and joint stiffnesses, re-solves, and returns the ordinary mutable model, ready for post-processing, force recovery, visualization or export.","title":"updatemodel(params, x) -> Model"},{"category":"section","location":"api/parameters/#Parameters","page":"Parameters","text":"The compiled optimization problem.","title":"Parameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameters/#AsapOptim.OptParams","page":"Parameters","text":"OptParams\n\nThe compiled optimization problem: everything constant during optimization, prepared once so each design evaluation is a handful of sparse products and one pure Asap solve.\n\nBuilt from a model and its design variables:\n\nOptParams(model, variables)\n\n(TrussOptParams and FrameOptParams are accepted aliases — the v1.0 core is unified, so one parameter type serves both.)\n\nFields (all constant per optimization)\n\nmodel::Model: the processed reference model (its cache holds the frozen sparsity pattern, DOF partition, and load vectors)\nvalues, lb, ub::Vector{Float64}: the design vector's start and bounds\nX0::Matrix{Float64}: base node positions (3 × n_nodes)\nSx::SparseMatrixCSC: position scatter — X(x) = X0 + reshape(Sx·x, 3, :) (spatial variables are ADDITIVE perturbations)\nA0::Vector{Float64}: per-element base areas (elements without an area variable keep these)\nSa::SparseMatrixCSC, amask::Vector{Bool}: area scatter — for elements with a variable, A(x) = Sa·x REPLACES the area (absolute semantics)\nP0v::Vector{Float64}, Sp::SparseMatrixCSC, prmask::Vector{Bool}, pmask::Vector{Bool}: flexural/torsional section-property scatter (rows 3(e−1)+k for Ix/Iy/J of element e) — same absolute semantics as areas; pmask flags elements with ANY such variable\nF::Vector{Float64}: the full-space load vector P − Pf at the BASE design (used as-is when loads = :frozen, or when the model has no element loads)\nP::Vector{Float64}: the full-space NODAL load vector (constant)\nloads::Symbol: :design (default) re-evaluates element-load fixed-end forces at every design (F(x) = P − Pf(x), Asap ≥ 1.2: line loads follow member length, self-weight ρA, condensed end moments the joint springs and EI); :frozen keeps F at the base design\nqa0::Vector{Float64}: per-element axial fixed-end force q̃₇ at the base design (the frozen axial-force correction)\nEload::SparseMatrixCSC: nel × nloads, 1 where load j sits on element e (cache.fef order) — sums per-load FEF terms per element\ni1, i2::Vector{Int}: per-element node indices; base_sections: the reference sections — plain-data mirrors so the differentiable path never reads mutable structs (a Zygote tangent-accumulation catastrophe at scale)\nCinc::SparseMatrixCSC: signed node-element incidence (nel × nnodes, −1 at the start node, +1 at the end) — element geometry (lengths, axial directions) evaluates as ONE matmul X · Cincᵀ instead of a per-element map (whose pullbacks would dominate gradient cost)\n\nDesign-evaluation contract\n\nx is the vector an optimizer manipulates; solve_structure(x, p) gives results, gradients flow through Zygote (or any ChainRules-aware engine) with no additional rules.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.OptParams"},{"category":"section","location":"api/networks/#Force-density-networks","page":"Force-density networks","text":"Differentiable form-finding over an Asap.Network.","title":"Force-density networks"},{"category":"type","location":"api/networks/#AsapOptim.NetworkOptParams","page":"Force-density networks","text":"NetworkOptParams\n\nCompiled force-density optimization problem over an Asap.Network (process!-ed): constant connectivity/loads/anchors plus the design-vector scatter for QVariables (grouping via CoupledVariable with an FDMelement target).\n\nThe FDM solve is linear in the free coordinates: (Cnᵀ diag(q) Cn) · xyz_free = Pn − Cnᵀ diag(q) Cf · xyz_fixed — evaluated purely by solve_network, differentiable end-to-end w.r.t. q.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.NetworkOptParams"},{"category":"method","location":"api/networks/#AsapOptim.NetworkOptParams-Tuple{Network, Vector{<:AbstractVariable}}","page":"Force-density networks","text":"NetworkOptParams(network::Asap.Network, variables::Vector{<:AbstractVariable})\n\nCompile a network and its QVariables (plus CoupledVariable groupings targeting FDMelements) into a NetworkOptParams: one design slot per independent variable, a sparse force-density scatter Sq, and constant copies of the connectivity, loads, and anchor positions. Processes the network first if needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.NetworkOptParams"},{"category":"type","location":"api/networks/#AsapOptim.NetworkOptResults","page":"Force-density networks","text":"NetworkOptResults\n\nOutputs of a force-density design evaluation: full nodal coordinates (X, Y, Z columns of the form-found geometry), the evaluated force densities Q, and member lengths L. Member forces are Q .* L.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.NetworkOptResults"},{"category":"type","location":"api/networks/#AsapOptim.QVariable","page":"Force-density networks","text":"QVariable <: AbstractVariable\n\nA force-density variable for FDM network optimization: the design entry REPLACES the element's force density q [force/length] (absolute semantics, like AreaVariable).\n\nQVariable(element::FDMelement, value, lb, ub)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.QVariable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/networks/#AsapOptim.member_forces-Tuple{NetworkOptResults}","page":"Force-density networks","text":"member_forces(res::NetworkOptResults) -> Vector\n\nFDM member forces [force]: q · L, tension-positive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.member_forces"},{"category":"method","location":"api/networks/#AsapOptim.solve_network-Tuple{AbstractVector, NetworkOptParams}","page":"Force-density networks","text":"solve_network(x, p::NetworkOptParams) -> NetworkOptResults\n\nEvaluate force densities: assemble the FDM system purely and solve for the free-node coordinates (Asap's solve_free multi-RHS adjoint carries the gradients). Differentiable w.r.t. x end-to-end.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.solve_network"},{"category":"section","location":"#AsapOptim.jl","page":"Home","text":"(Image: Gradient field of a spaceframe)\n\nHigh-performance, general structural optimization in the Asap.jl environment.\n\nAsapOptim maps a design vector to a differentiable structural analysis and back. The vector holds node positions, cross-section properties, connection stiffnesses and force densities. You write ordinary Julia objective and constraint functions. Gradients flow end-to-end through the solve by automatic differentiation, and any gradient-based optimizer (Nonconvex.jl, NLopt, Optim, your own loop) drives the design.\n\nAsapOptim is a thin layer over Asap's differentiable core. It compiles design variables into sparse scatter maps and evaluates designs through Asap's pure, AD-transparent solve path. It re-implements no analysis and contains no hand-written adjoints. One unified core handles trusses, frames, semi-rigid joints, element loads and mixed models.","title":"AsapOptim.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"pkg> add AsapOptim\n\nFor the examples you will also want an AD engine and an optimizer:\n\npkg> add Zygote Nonconvex NonconvexNLopt","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-workflow","page":"Home","text":"Every optimization follows the same five steps:\n\nModel: build and solve an Asap.Model as usual.\nVariables: declare what the optimizer controls (Design variables).\nParameters: compile the model and variables into an OptParams, once.\nObjective and constraints: plain functions of solve_structure(x, params) results, or of GeometricProperties(x, params) when no solve is needed.\nOptimize: hand the functions and params.lb/params.ub to your optimizer, then recover the final design with updatemodel(params, x_optimal).\n\nusing Asap, AsapOptim\nusing Zygote                       # activates Asap's ChainRules extension\n\nvars   = AbstractVariable[ ... ]   # step 2\nparams = OptParams(model, vars)    # step 3\nx0     = copy(params.values)\n\nobjective(x) = compliance(solve_structure(x, params), params)   # step 4\n\nvalue, gradient = Zygote.withgradient(objective, x0)            # step 5: any gradient-based optimizer from here\n\nStart with the Quick start.","title":"The workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"#Package-overview","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Package overview"},{"category":"module","location":"#AsapOptim","page":"Home","text":"AsapOptim\n\nStructural optimization on top of Asap's differentiable core.\n\nThe v1.0 rewrite: Asap itself now provides the pure, AD-transparent assembly/solve path (ModelState → Asap.solve), so this package no longer re-implements analysis or hand-writes adjoints — it is a thin, Zygote-free layer that maps a design vector to a ModelState and back:\n\ndesign variables (SpatialVariable, AreaVariable, CoupledVariable) declare what the optimizer controls\nOptParams compiles them into sparse scatter maps\nsolve_structure (aliases solve_truss/solve_frame) evaluates a design: positions/sections → pure solve → OptResults\naxial_force/axial_stress/compliance are pure functions of the results, differentiable end-to-end\nGeometricProperties gives geometry-only quantities (lengths, areas) without a solve\nupdatemodel materializes an optimal design back into a solved Asap.Model\n\nBring your own AD engine and optimizer: loading Zygote (or anything using ChainRulesCore) activates Asap's rule extension automatically.\n\nThe force-density Network optimization path (QVariable → NetworkOptParams → solve_network) rides the same pure machinery.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim"},{"category":"section","location":"api/implicit/#Implicit-differentiation","page":"Implicit differentiation","text":"Exact solution tangents and constraint Jacobians by the implicit-function theorem.","title":"Implicit differentiation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/implicit/#AsapOptim.DesignTangents","page":"Implicit differentiation","text":"DesignTangents\n\nSolution of solution_tangents: the design evaluation res (an OptResults) plus the full solution tangent matrix dU (ndofs × nvars, dU[:, j] = ∂U/∂xⱼ). Feed to axial_force_jacobian/axial_stress_jacobian, or slice dU directly for displacement-constraint rows (e.g. vertical displacements: t.dU[3:6:end, :]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.DesignTangents"},{"category":"method","location":"api/implicit/#AsapOptim.axial_force_jacobian-Tuple{DesignTangents, OptParams}","page":"Implicit differentiation","text":"axial_force_jacobian(t::DesignTangents, p::OptParams) -> Matrix\n\nExact Jacobian ∂N/∂x (nelements × nvars) of the per-element axial forces, chaining the solution tangents with the closed-form geometry terms (N = EA·(ΔX·ΔU)/L² differentiated in U, X, and A).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.axial_force_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/implicit/#AsapOptim.axial_stress_jacobian-Tuple{DesignTangents, OptParams}","page":"Implicit differentiation","text":"axial_stress_jacobian(t::DesignTangents, p::OptParams) -> Matrix\n\nExact Jacobian ∂σ/∂x (nelements × nvars) of the per-element axial stresses σ = N/A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.axial_stress_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/implicit/#AsapOptim.solution_tangents-Tuple{AbstractVector{Float64}, OptParams}","page":"Implicit differentiation","text":"solution_tangents(x, p::OptParams) -> DesignTangents\n\nEvaluate the design AND the exact solution tangents ∂U/∂x by implicit differentiation: one assembly, one factorization (through p.solver), one multi-RHS back-substitution — instead of one Dual re-assembly per forward-mode chunk. Equal to the ForwardDiff/Zygote Jacobian of the displacement field to machine precision, at a fraction of the cost (the per-iteration workhorse for constraint Jacobians in optimization loops).\n\nPlain Float64 only — this path is for optimizer callbacks and is not itself AD-transparent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AsapOptim.solution_tangents"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/networks/#Force-density-networks","page":"Force-density networks","text":"The same variables → parameters → solve pattern works on Asap.Network (force-density form-finding). solve_network is differentiable in the force densities end to end:\n\nqv = QVariable(network.elements[1], 1.0, 0.1, 10.0)\nvars = AbstractVariable[qv; [CoupledVariable(el, qv) for el in network.elements[2:5]]]\n\nnparams = NetworkOptParams(network, vars)\n\nfunction obj(x)\n    res = solve_network(x, nparams)          # NetworkOptResults: X, Y, Z, Q, L\n    sum(abs2, member_forces(res))            # forces are Q .* L, tension-positive\nend\n\nZygote.gradient(obj, copy(nparams.values))\n\nNetworkOptParams rejects mixed (per-axis) node fixity: the differentiable force-density path solves all three coordinates against one partition.","title":"Force-density networks"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Migrating-from-v0.1","page":"Migrating from v0.1","text":"TrussOptParams and FrameOptParams still work. They are aliases of the unified OptParams, and solve_truss/solve_frame alias solve_structure.\nres.U is the full 6-DOF-per-node vector for every model (v0.1's truss path was 3-DOF): vertical displacements are U[2:6:end], not U[2:3:end].\nResults carry positions as a 3 × n matrix res.X instead of separate X/Y/Z vectors.\nThe load vector of an evaluated design is res.F (with element loads it varies with the design); params.F is the base-design vector. Or use compliance(res, params).\nCustom rrules, the functional re-assembly path and the truss/frame code split are gone: differentiability comes from Asap's core. The old implementation is preserved, unloaded, in legacy_v0/ for reference.\nSection parameterization is available through SectionVariable (:A, :Ix, :Iy, :J).","title":"Migrating from v0.1"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Quick-start:-geometry-optimization","page":"Quick start","text":"The runnable files for this page are in the repository's examples/ folder. Consider this truss:\n\n(Image: Problem formulation)\n\nBuilt with Asap:\n\nusing Asap\n\nsteel = Material(200e6, 80.0, 0.3)        # E [force/length²], ρ, ν\nsection = Section(steel, 0.01)            # axial-only, A = 0.01\n\nL = 10.0    # span\nnx = 12     # bays\ndy = 1.0    # depth\nload = 75.0 # per-node load\ndx = L / nx\n\nbottom_nodes = [Node([x, 0.0, 0.0], :free, :bottom) for x in range(0, L, nx + 1)]\nfixnode!(first(bottom_nodes), :pinned)\nfixnode!(last(bottom_nodes), :xfree)\n\ntop_nodes = [Node([dx / 2 + dx * (i - 1), dy, 0.0], :free, :top) for i in 1:nx]\n\nbottom_elements = [TrussElement(bottom_nodes[i], bottom_nodes[i+1], section, :bottom) for i in 1:nx]\ntop_elements = [TrussElement(top_nodes[i], top_nodes[i+1], section, :top) for i in 1:nx-1]\nweb_elements = [\n    [TrussElement(nb, nt, section, :web) for (nb, nt) in zip(bottom_nodes[1:end-1], top_nodes)];\n    [TrussElement(nt, nb, section, :web) for (nt, nb) in zip(top_nodes, bottom_nodes[2:end])]\n]\n\nnodes = [bottom_nodes; top_nodes]\nelements = AbstractElement{Float64}[bottom_elements; top_elements; web_elements]\nloads = AbstractLoad{Float64}[NodeForce(node, [0.0, -load, 0.0]) for node in nodes[:bottom]]\n\nmodel = Model(nodes, elements, loads)\nplanarize!(model)\nsolve!(model)\n\n(Image: Initial design)\n\nTo find the minimum-compliance (maximum-stiffness) geometry, let every top-chord node move in x and y. A SpatialVariable is an additive offset from the modeled position, so 0.0 starts from the current geometry:\n\nusing AsapOptim\nusing Zygote\n\nxmin, xmax = (-1, 1) .* dx ./ 2 .* 0.9\nymin, ymax = -0.9dy, dy\n\nvars = AbstractVariable[\n    [SpatialVariable(node, 0.0, xmin, xmax, :X) for node in model.nodes[:top]];\n    [SpatialVariable(node, 0.0, ymin, ymax, :Y) for node in model.nodes[:top]]\n]\n\nparams = OptParams(model, vars)\nx0 = copy(params.values)      # the optimizer's starting point\nlength(x0)\n24\n\nThe objective is one line. solve_structure evaluates a design, and compliance reduces it to the scalar Fᵀu:\n\nOBJ = x -> compliance(solve_structure(x, params), params)\n\nvalue, grad = Zygote.withgradient(OBJ, x0)   # value and exact gradient, no extra setup\nvalue\n7.714179687500055\n\nOptimize with anything gradient-based. Here is L-BFGS through Nonconvex.jl:\n\nusing Nonconvex, NonconvexNLopt\n\noptmodel = Nonconvex.Model(OBJ)\naddvar!(optmodel, params.lb, params.ub)\n\nres = optimize(optmodel, NLoptAlg(:LD_LBFGS), x0,\n    options = NLoptOptions(maxeval = 500, maxtime = 60))\n\nmodel2 = updatemodel(params, res.minimizer)   # a solved Asap.Model of the optimum\n\n(Image: Optimized design)\n\nNext: constrain the problem and size the members too (Constrained optimization and AD), or see every variable type (Design variables).","title":"Quick start: geometry optimization"}]
}
